# How fast did the world's population grow to 8 billion?
subtitle: "Draw how the world's population grew from 1950 to today, then see how each new billion arrived faster, and where the curve is heading."

## How fast did the world's population grow to 8 billion?
text: {"font_size":34}
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1800
      - 1804
      - 1850
      - 1900
      - 1927
      - 1950
      - 1960
      - 1974
      - 1987
      - 1999
      - 2011
      - 2022
      - 2024
      - 2050
      - 2084
      - 2100
    series:
      - {name: World, values: [[0.98, 1, 1.26, 1.65, 2, 2.5, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null], [0.98, 1, 1.26, 1.65, 2, 2.5, 3.02, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.16, null, null, null]]}
      - {name: UN forecast, values: [[null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null], [null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, 8.16, 9.66, 10.29, 10.18]]}
    stage: 0
    x_ticks: false
    xlim:
      - 1800
      - 2100
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 11
    y_label: Billions of people
    x_label: Year
sources:
    - id: un
      title: World population milestones / Day of Eight Billion
      authors: United Nations
      year: 2022
      url: https://www.un.org/en/dayof8billion
      finding: 1 billion about 1804, 2 billion 1927, 3 billion 1960, 4 billion 1974, 7 billion 2011, 8 billion on 15 November 2022
    - id: owid
      title: Population
      authors: Our World in Data (HYDE, UN WPP)
      year: 2024
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/population-growth
      finding: world population about 1.65 billion in 1900, 2.5 billion in 1950, 6.15 billion in 2000, 8.16 billion in 2024
    - id: wpp
      title: World Population Prospects 2024
      authors: United Nations
      year: 2024
      finding: world population is projected to reach 9.66 billion in 2050 and peak at about 10.3 billion in the mid-2080s, 10.2 billion in 2100

    card title "How fast did we reach 8 billion?"

How did the world's population grow to eight billion?
    draw axes

In 1800 there were about one billion of us. By 1950, about two and a half billion.
    draw line_1

    ask "Here is the line up to 1950. Draw the rest: how did it grow to today?" on line_1 from 1950 store g
        right: Close. You drew {g}; the real line ends at {g.true} billion.
        wrong: You drew {g}; the real line ends at {g.true} billion.

    animate stage 1 duration 1.8

For all of human history until about 1804, we never reached one billion.
    dot p1 #2f6b8f at.data [1804,1]
    text p1_l "1 bn: 1804" above-right p1 gap 14 #2f6b8f font_size 34

The second billion took a hundred and twenty-three years, until 1927.
    dot p2 #2f6b8f at.data [1927,2]
    text p2_l "2 bn: 1927" above-left p2 gap 14 #2f6b8f font_size 34

Then the billions came faster: three in 1960, four in 1974, and the eighth in 2022,
    dot p8 #2f6b8f at.data [2022,8]
    text p8_l "8 bn: 2022" left p8 gap 14 #b5482e font_size 34

only eleven years after the seventh.
    highlight p8_l

Not because people had more children, but because far fewer died young.
    highlight line_1

And the curve is already bending: the United Nations expects a peak of about ten billion in the 2080s.
    draw line_2
---
